these are the last few images taken by a  NASA spacecraft seconds before it's  smashed into a 5 million tonne asteroid  [Applause]  you can tell by the celebrations back on  Earth the crash was no accident  the dart mission is the first test of a  potential planetary defense system to  deflect dangerous asteroids from our  home planet  NASA chose their target carefully a  small asteroid orbiting another seven  million miles from Earth  Dart approached the asteroid pair Didi  moss and dimorphos at nearly 15 000  miles an hour it aimed for head-on  collision with a 160 meter wide  dimorphos cameras on a buddy satellite  called leecher Cube recorded the impact  and The Cloud of debris The Hope is the  crash will slow dimorphoses orbit but it  will take months of observations to  prove it has worked  immediately they'll be looking at how  much debris is ejected from the asteroid  after impact the more stuff we come out  the more we move and the campaign to  understand that and how much we've  really uh you know how much we've moved  it will happen over the next year and  there's actually telescopes all across  the globe that are going to be looking  at uh dinamos and dimorphous and trying  to understand how much we've changed uh  over the next year if an asteroid the  size of dimorphos were to hit Earth it  could destroy a city it's not the size  that this you know going to end life on  the planet but it's large enough that it  could cause a significant problem  if one that size was ever discovered  coming towards Earth and so being able  to do something about that seems like a  very sensible technology to have no  known asteroid large and 140 meters is  expected to collide with Earth in The  Next Century but only 40 percent of the  smaller asteroids have been found  so if dark proves to have been a success  more campaigns to study and track space  rocks will be needed to spot danger  early enough to leave enough time to  knock it safely out of the way  Tom Clark Sky News
 

